{"name":"Allegory","hex":"#b4b2a9","fetched":"2026-10-20","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"allegory","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The use of symbols which may be interpreted to reveal a hidden, broader message, usually a moral or political one, about real-world issues and occurrences; also, the interpretation of such symbols.","A picture, story, or other form of communication in which one or more characters, events, or places are used to reveal a hidden, broader message about real-world issues and occurrences."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Synonym of allegorize."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/allegory"}],"entries":[{"title":"Allegory","blocks":[{"text":"As a literary device or artistic form, an allegory is any narrative or visual work of art intended as symbolism for moral or political ideas. Typically, the depicted events and characters serve as the symbols for such ideas. Creators have used allegory throughout history in all forms of art to illustrate or convey complex ideas and concepts in ways that are comprehensible or striking to its viewers, readers, or listeners."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegory","wikidata":"Q18535"},{"title":"Allegory (mathematics)","blocks":[{"text":"In category theory, an allegory is a category that has some of the structure of the category Rel of sets and binary relations between them. Allegories can be used as an abstraction of categories of relations, and in this sense the theory of allegories is a generalization of relation algebra to relations between different sorts. Allegories are also useful in defining and investigating certain constructions in category theory, such as exact completions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Allegory_(mathematics)","wikidata":"Q4731385"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Salvator_Rosa_%28Italian%29_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Salvator Rosa: Allegory of Fortune, representing Fortuna, the goddess of luck, with the horn of plenty","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalvator_Rosa_(Italian)_-_Allegory_of_Fortune_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Pedro_I_of_Brazil_and_Charles_Stuart_.jpg/500px-Pedro_I_of_Brazil_and_Charles_Stuart_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Pedro_I_of_Brazil_and_Charles_Stuart_.jpg/500px-Pedro_I_of_Brazil_and_Charles_Stuart_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Allegory of the recognition of the Empire of Brazil and its independence. The painting depicts British diplomat Sir Charles Stuart presenting his letter of credence to Emperor Pedro I of Brazil, who is flanked by his wife Maria Leopoldina, their daughter Maria da Glória (later Queen of Portugal as Maria II), and other dignitaries. At right, a winged figure, representing History, carving the \"great event\" on a stone tablet.[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APedro_I_of_Brazil_and_Charles_Stuart_.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Marco_Marcola_-_Mitolo%C5%A1ka